  "post": {
    "caption": "Miners reportedly threw away Montana's sapphires. In 1895, gold prospectors kept finding blue pebbles in Yogo Gulch, in the Little Belt Mountains of central Montana. A cigar box of them reached Tiffany's gem expert, George Kunz, who reportedly called them the finest gems found in America. Most sapphires are heated, but Yogos usually need no heat treatment. They come straight out of a thin dike of igneous rock, the crystals are small and flat, and a cut stone over two carats is extremely rare. Yogo is often called Montana's most famous sapphire mine, and Montana named the sapphire a state gem in 1969.",
